  "summary": "Answer the office phone, take a message, and say who is away, travelling or coming back with the active participle: مُسَافِر، ذَاهِب، عَائِد.",
  "intro": "Mrs Huda is travelling, and Daniel has to answer the office phone. To say what someone is doing right now, Arabic often uses the active participle, which works like an adjective: هِيَ مُسَافِرَة (she is travelling), أَنَا ذَاهِب (I'm going), هُوَ عَائِد غَدًا (he's coming back tomorrow). It agrees with the person: ذَاهِب for a man, ذَاهِبَة for a woman.",
  "objectives": [
    "Answer the phone and say who is speaking",
    "Say that someone is away, travelling or coming back with active participles",
    "Take a message, or offer to take one",
    "Promise to call someone back with أَتَّصِلُ بِكَ"
  ],
